<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs exclusions. Keep this file short and every entry justified — an exclusion nobody can defend
  is how static analysis stops finding anything.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

  <!--
    EI_EXPOSE_REP / EI_EXPOSE_REP2 on the data records.

    These packages are records that hold Map and List components and are serialised to JSON immediately.
    Every value put into them is already immutable — Map.of, List.of, List.copyOf,
    Collections.unmodifiableMap — but SpotBugs reasons about the *declared* type, so it reports each of
    the 60 accessors and constructors as exposing internal state.

    Copying defensively in each one would add sixty pieces of ceremony that copy an immutable collection
    into another immutable collection. The invariant is enforced where the values are created
    (LanguageMap, DocumentRecord's compact constructor) rather than at every boundary they cross.

    Scoped to these packages deliberately: anywhere that holds genuinely mutable state, this check
    should still fire.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Or>
      <Package name="cz.mzk.iiif.presentation"/>
      <Package name="cz.mzk.iiif.spi"/>
      <Package name="cz.mzk.iiif.kramerius"/>
    </Or>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    cz.mzk.iiif.document is deliberately narrower: EI_EXPOSE_REP only, not EI_EXPOSE_REP2.

    DocumentRecord is filled by adapters from live Solr responses, so the maps handed to it really are
    caller-owned and mutable. It copies them — LanguageMap.immutable, an unmodifiable LinkedHashMap that
    keeps insertion order so the serialised bytes stay stable — and that fixed five of the eight reports
    honestly. The three that remain are accessors returning those already-immutable maps, which SpotBugs
    cannot see because it reasons about the declared Map type.

    Leaving EI_EXPOSE_REP2 active here is the point: it is what would catch the next record that takes a
    collection and forgets to copy it.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Package name="cz.mzk.iiif.document"/>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    cz.mzk.iiif.adapter, on the same terms and for the same reason.

    Two records here carry a slice of a Solr walk — the rows, and the cursor to carry on from. Both copy
    their list in a compact constructor, which is what fixed the EI_EXPOSE_REP2 reports honestly; what
    remains is the accessor handing back a List whose immutability SpotBugs cannot see.

    EI_EXPOSE_REP2 stays active for this package deliberately. It is the direction that matters — a
    record taking a caller-owned mutable collection and keeping it — and it is what will catch the next
    one that forgets to copy.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Package name="cz.mzk.iiif.adapter"/>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Injected collaborators, not data.

    A SizeStore is a service handed to a component at construction — a database-backed one in
    production, a fake in tests. "Defensively copying" it is not a thing that can be done, and holding
    the caller's instance is the entire point of passing it. SpotBugs reports every such field because
    the declared type is an interface it cannot prove immutable.

    Scoped to this one type rather than to the packages, so an ordinary mutable field slipping into the
    same class is still reported.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Field type="cz.mzk.iiif.gateway.store.SizeStore"/>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
